# Vulnerability details

## Impact

In the BigBang market, when users repay USDO, it only burns a portion of USD0. The remaining amount (interest accrued) can be collected by the owner as fees at a later time.
```solidity
function _repay(
    address from,
    address to,
    uint256 part
) internal returns (uint256 amount) {
    (totalBorrow, amount) = totalBorrow.sub(part, true);

    userBorrowPart[to] -= part;

    uint256 toWithdraw = (amount - part); //acrrued
    uint256 toBurn = amount - toWithdraw;
    yieldBox.withdraw(assetId, from, address(this), amount, 0);
    //burn USDO
    if (toBurn > 0) { // @audit Only burn toBurn ?
        IUSDOBase(address(asset)).burn(address(this), toBurn);
    }

    emit LogRepay(from, to, amount, part);
}
```
The issue is that the calculation in the `_repay()` function is incorrect. The `toBurn` amount is not the amount without the interest accrued, but rather the share in `totalBorrow`. As a result, if an attacker borrows any amount and then repays immediately (in the same block/TX), the calculation still sets `toWithdraw > 0`, which means there is some interest accrued but in fact there is not. The result is that if the attacker repeats this process, an infinite amount of USD0 will be minted as fees in `refreshPenroseFees()`.

## Proof of Concept

Consider the following scenario:

1. Currently, `totalBorrow` state is `elastic = 120, base = 100`.
2. Alice borrows `amount = 60` then `part = 50` and is recorded as `userBorrowPart[Alice] = 50`.
3. Alice repays `part = 50` in the same TX. Obviously, there is no interest accrued yet, but in the `_repay()` function, it is still calculated as `toWithdraw = 60 - 50 = 10`, which means there are `10 USD0` just minted from nowhere and considered as fees that can be collected later.

