# Vulnerability details

## Description

The AirdropTapOption struct in the AOTAP contract has a expiry field that is a uint128. This means that the maximum value of the expiry field is 2^128 - 1. However, the mintedAOTAP variable in the contract is a uint256, which has a maximum value of 2^256 - 1. This means that if the number of AOTAPs minted reaches 2^256 - 1, the expiry field of the next AOTAP minted will overflow.

For example, if there are currently 2^256 - 1 AOTAPs minted, and the next AOTAP is minted with an expiry timestamp of 2^128 - 1, the expiry timestamp of the next AOTAP will be 0.

## Impact
This bug could allow an attacker to mint an AOTAP with an invalid expiry timestamp. This could allow the attacker to claim an option that has expired.

## Proof of Concept
The following code can be used to reproduce the bug:

contract AOTAP {
    uint256 public mintedAOTAP;

    function mint(address _to, uint128 _expiry) public {
        mintedAOTAP++;
        _safeMint(_to, mintedAOTAP);
    }
}

contract Attacker {
    function exploit() public {
        AOTAP aotap = new AOTAP();
        aotap.mint(address(this), 2^128 - 1);
    }
}

This contract will create a new instance of the AOTAP contract and mint an AOTAP with an expiry timestamp of 2^128 - 1. The expiry timestamp of the next AOTAP minted will be 0.

## Tools Used
Remix IDE
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
The expiry field in the AirdropTapOption struct should be changed to a uint256. This would prevent the expiry timestamp from overflowing.


