The function `newEpoch` in the contract `TapiocaOptionBroker.sol` can be called to calculate the rewards to be emitted for the week / epoch. The epoch times are updated, and then the tapOFT contract's `emitForWeek` function is called to calculate the number of tokens to be emitted. These emissions are then transferred to the respective gauges for distribution.

```solidity
// Extract TAP
uint256 epochTAP = tapOFT.emitForWeek();
_emitToGauges(epochTAP);
```

The amount passed to `_emitgauges` is determined by the return value of `emitForWeek`. Also note that this function can only be called once per epoch. Thus if the function `emitForWeek` returns a bad value or zero, that will deny rewards for the whole epoch.

The main issue lies with the function `emitForWeek` in the `TapOFT.sol` contract. This function calculates the amount of tap tokens to be emitted for the week. But this function can be called by anyone since it has no access control, and has an early exit clause which returns zero if the function has already been called for the week.

```solidity
uint256 week = _timestampToWeek(block.timestamp);
if (emissionForWeek[week] > 0) return 0;
// Other logic
uint256 emission = uint256(_computeEmission());
emission += unclaimed;
emissionForWeek[week] = emission;
```

Thus any user can call this function **before** the `newEpoch` function is called in the `TapiocaOptionBroker.sol` contract. This would set a non zero value to the `emissionForWeek` mapping for the week. Eventually when `newEpoch` is called on the option broker, the `emitForWeek` function will return zero since `emissionForWeek` for the week is already non-zero. Thus no emissions will be set for the current epoch.

Since this breaks the emission schedule, this is classified as a high severity issue.

