# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Users can borrow 10x as much as they should be able to relative to their collateral, allowing attackers to drain the protocol.

## Proof of Concept
In BigBang, `collateralizationRate` is set as 750% or 75000 bps, but is intended to be set as 75% or 7500 bps.

```solidity
collateralizationRate = 75000; // 75%
```

As a result, when solvency is checked after execution of borrow functions or before liquidation, a user will only be flagged as insolvent if their borrow position is greater than 750% of the size of their collateral position.

```solidity
function _isSolvent(
	address user,
	uint256 _exchangeRate
) internal view returns (bool) {
	// accrue must have already been called!
	uint256 borrowPart = userBorrowPart[user];
	if (borrowPart == 0) return true;
	uint256 collateralShare = userCollateralShare[user];
	if (collateralShare == 0) return false;

	Rebase memory _totalBorrow = totalBorrow;

	return
		yieldBox.toAmount(
			collateralId,
			collateralShare *
				(EXCHANGE_RATE_PRECISION / FEE_PRECISION) *
				collateralizationRate, // @audit: incorrect collateralizationRate returns true for up to 750% collateralization
			false
		) >=
		// Moved exchangeRate here instead of dividing the other side to preserve more precision
		(borrowPart * _totalBorrow.elastic * _exchangeRate) /
			_totalBorrow.base;
}
```

Because of this, attackers can simply borrow up to 750% of their collateral and run away with the tokens without any need to retrieve their collateral. Of course, this process can also be repeated until the protocol is drained.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
`collateralizationRate` should be set as `7500`.


