# Vulnerability details
Magnetar is a multi-purpose helper contract designed to provide a simplified and consistent access point for users to interact with the broader Tapioca protocol.  `MagnetarV2#_permit()` is intended to allow users to call `permit()` on any contract that supports it. 

However, as written the `_permit()` function allows for an attacker to call any function on any contract with Magnetar as `msg.sender`, with the lone restriction that the first argument passed in the target call must be the same as the attacker's address.

`MagnetarV2#_permit()` is a private function that is only accessible through a call to Magnetar's multicall funciton, `burst()`. The attacker needs to provide a call with an "id" equal to 1 or 2 (`PERMIT_ALL` or `PERMIT`), a target contract to call, and calldata for the call he'd like to make. Note that it doesn't matter if we use PERMIT_ALl or PERMIT - the attack is the same.

```solidity
function burst(
        Call[] calldata calls
    ) external payable returns (Result[] memory returnData) {
        ...
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < length; i++) {
            Call calldata _action = calls[i];
            ...
            if (_action.id == PERMIT_ALL) { // Either of these will enter _permit
                _permit(
                    _action.target, // All parameters here attacker-controlled
                    _action.call,
                    true,
                    _action.allowFailure
                );
            } else if (_action.id == PERMIT) {
                _permit(
                    _action.target,
                    _action.call,
                    false,
                    _action.allowFailure
                );
            }
}
