## Impact
In both `TapiocaOptionBroker` and `AirdropBroker`, payment amount for options can be heavily reduced due to division rounding;
This constitutes a net loss for the protocol's treasury 

## Proof of Concept
The function `_getDiscountedPaymentAmount` computing the amount of tokens that a user has to pay to exercise an oTap option, does not follow the multiplication before division rule:

```solidity
/// @notice Computes the discounted payment amount for a given OTC amount in USD
/// @param _otcAmountInUSD The OTC amount in USD, 18 decimals
/// @param _paymentTokenValuation The payment token valuation in USD, 18 decimals
/// @param _discount The discount in BPS
/// @param _paymentTokenDecimals The payment token decimals
/// @return paymentAmount The discounted payment amount

function _getDiscountedPaymentAmount(
    uint256 _otcAmountInUSD,
    uint256 _paymentTokenValuation,
    uint256 _discount,
    uint256 _paymentTokenDecimals
) internal pure returns (uint256 paymentAmount) {
    // Calculate payment amount
    uint256 rawPaymentAmount = _otcAmountInUSD / _paymentTokenValuation;
    paymentAmount =
        rawPaymentAmount -
        muldiv(rawPaymentAmount, _discount, 100e4); // 1e4 is discount decimals, 100 is discount percentage
    paymentAmount = paymentAmount / (10 ** (18 - _paymentTokenDecimals));
}
```

This means that the `paymentAmount` returned can be very low compared to the ideal price.
Since _otcAmountInUSD is 18 decimals and _paymentTokenValuation is also 18 decimals, it is enough that `_paymentTokenValuation > rawPaymentAmount` for paymentAmount to be zero, and the buyer gets options for free.

