# Vulnerability details

Both the airdrop broker and the standard TapOFT broker are following very unsafe patterns. The `brokerClaim()` function can be called by anyone. Unless this function is called atomically with the deployment of this smart contract itself through another smart contract, front-running with a malicious contract is too high a risk, and quite feasible. The role can only be assigned once (with the rare exception of a block reorg event) meaning it's irreversible.

```
    /// @notice Claim the Broker role of the oTAP contract
    function oTAPBrokerClaim() external {
        oTAP.brokerClaim();
    }
```

```
    /// @notice tOB claim
    function brokerClaim() external {
        require(broker == address(0), "OTAP: only once");
        broker = msg.sender;
    }
```

## Impact
Acquiring the broker role in the [aoTAP](https://github.com/Tapioca-DAO/tap-token-audit/blob/main/contracts/option-airdrop/aoTAP.sol) and [oTAP]() contracts gives the deployer the ability to issue as many aoTAP/oTAP tokens as they wish, completely compromising the token allocation if snatched by an attacker.

## Proof of Concept

1) A malicious actor deploys a slightly modified version of the broker contract
2) The broker contract is deployed, and the malicious actor backruns the deployment calling `brokerClaim()` through their own compromised contract
3) The contract can operate as intended, however when users exercise their options, they pay the malicious contract the payment token and earn no `TAP` tokens back

This is but one example of an attack pattern, the malicious actor has a lot of freedom to choose on how to act as soon as they acquire the role. 

